00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:17,800
 So I want to hand this over to Bee

264
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,760
 who has really been at the front lines

265
00:11:19,760 --> 00:11:21,680
 of crypto policy on a number of fronts.

266
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:23,800
 But before I do, I want to remind everyone

267
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:26,120
 how a bill becomes a law.

268
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,600
 It's introduced.

269
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:31,400
 It is referred and reviewed by committees.

270
00:11:31,400 --> 00:11:36,120
 There is a floor debate and a vote in both chambers.

271
00:11:36,120 --> 00:11:38,280
 There is a conference committee

272
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:39,600
 that works out of compromise

273
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:41,680
 if there's different versions.

274
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:43,480
 There's presidential action.

275
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:44,520
 We're all familiar with that.

276
00:11:44,520 --> 00:11:45,360
 He can sign it.

277
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:46,280
 He can veto it.

278
00:11:46,280 --> 00:11:48,120
 And then the law is implemented.

279
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:49,880
 So where are we with a markup

280
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:52,760
 and what does actually a markup need?

281
00:11:52,760 --> 00:11:57,040
 It actually means that the bill is formerly debated,

282
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:00,120
 amended, reviewed by the committee

283
00:12:00,120 --> 00:12:02,880
 before it can move forward in the lawmaking process.

284
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:04,480
 And the reason this is important

285
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:09,360
 is if the markup goes badly, the bill can die.

286
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:12,960
 The markup is also kind of in many ways the last chance

287
00:12:12,960 --> 00:12:16,480
 to make and consider any changes to the bill.

288
00:12:16,480 --> 00:12:19,400
 So the big news this week was we were supposed to have

289
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:21,920
 both a financial services committee markup

290
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:25,480
 and an agriculture to separate committee markups.

291
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:29,800
 On Jan 15th, AG has punted into later in the month,

292
00:12:29,800 --> 00:12:31,720
 financial services is moving forward.

293
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:35,080
 And now we're all looking at this revised bill.

294
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:37,680
 Some parts of crypto are very happy with parts of it.

295
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:39,440
 Other parts are very upset.

296
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:44,520
 One big, big point of contention is defy the, tell us more.

297
00:12:44,520 --> 00:12:49,160
 - Yeah, so there's a lot of new stuff in this bill.

298
00:12:49,160 --> 00:12:52,360
 But I wanted to focus on defy in particular

299
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:54,400
 just because there's a lot of new stuff

300
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,080
 in that section in particular.

301
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:58,920
 And it's just been one of the most hotly debated parts

302
00:12:58,920 --> 00:12:59,760
 of the bill.

303
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:04,400
 So I'll just start out by saying or acknowledging

304
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,400
 that like Congress has a really hard job, right?

305
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:09,840
 There's I think always this tension

306
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:14,760
 between what you enshrine into the statute

307
00:13:14,760 --> 00:13:18,520
 and what you leave to regulators to deal with

308
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:21,720
 or what you just don't enforce or don't regulate at all, right?

309
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:24,960
 And I think it's really hard to strike the right balance,

310
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:28,480
 especially when you're talking about a technology

311
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:33,080
 that is very new and also still evolving sometimes quickly, right?

312
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:36,200
 So I think, you know, they have a very hard job

313
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:39,680
 and nowhere is that more like acute to me

314
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:41,520
 than in the defy section.

315
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:44,360
 So again, we've all had very limited time

316
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:45,760
 to review the new language,

317
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:47,760
 but I did want to give some initial thoughts

318
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:49,720
 just on what I'm seeing.

319
00:13:49,720 --> 00:13:51,160
 So at a high level,

320
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:53,800
 the draft is trying to draw a bright line

321
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:58,200
 between decentralized protocols and infrastructure

322
00:13:58,200 --> 00:14:01,760
 and protocols or parties where, you know,

323
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:04,760
 there's some sort of control over trading,

324
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:07,760
 execution, custody, or access.

325
00:14:07,760 --> 00:14:11,920
 And that side would potentially be regulated

326
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:13,320
 as brokers, exchanges,

327
00:14:13,320 --> 00:14:16,800
 or some sort of regulated financial intermediary.

328
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:20,840
 So all of it really comes down to this concept of control.

329
00:14:20,840 --> 00:14:23,560
 But I think the difficulty here,

330
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:24,920
 and it's something that, you know,

331
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:28,760
 we as crypto lawyers have been thinking about for years,

332
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:33,160
 this concept of control is actually really hard to define, right?

333
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:36,640
 So the way that the current language tries to define it

334
00:14:36,640 --> 00:14:39,000
 is, I would say like in three different ways.

335
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:43,840
 One is, is there discretion over trading or execution, right?

336
00:14:43,840 --> 00:14:47,200
 So do you route trades, do you match counter parties?

337
00:14:47,200 --> 00:14:49,080
 Do you decide execution timing?

338
00:14:49,080 --> 00:14:51,400
 Do you modify prices or priority?

339
00:14:51,400 --> 00:14:55,960
 Do you intervene in execution outside of like pre-coded rules?

340
00:14:55,960 --> 00:14:58,600
 Another way they define control is,

341
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:02,080
 do you have unilateral authority to do things

342
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:05,960
 like alter issuance or distribution parameters

343
00:15:05,960 --> 00:15:08,200
 to change execution logic,

344
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:10,400
 to restrict access to the protocol?

345
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:12,560
 That's a really important one that we'll come back to.

346
00:15:12,560 --> 00:15:14,000
 Or to pause?

347
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,720
 Sorry to interrupt, but one important thing about this,

348
00:15:16,720 --> 00:15:18,240
 and you can confirm your thoughts

349
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:19,920
 as you're talking about control,

350
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,200
 is one of the things that I see in this bill

